WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.037
Chapter one: how Scholium works.

00:00:02.037 --> 00:00:05.347
Scholium is a network for preprints and peer review.

00:00:05.347 --> 00:00:12.304
Authors publish free, reviewers review in the open, and every reader can check that a paper is genuine.

00:00:12.304 --> 00:00:15.882
A scholium is a note in the margin of a scholarly text.

00:00:15.882 --> 00:00:23.243
On Scholium, a paper lives together with its reviews, annotations and corrections, in the margin beside it.

00:00:23.243 --> 00:00:27.803
Six ideas are behind Scholium. We will look at each idea in turn.

00:00:27.803 --> 00:00:30.408
Idea one: everything is a signed record.

00:00:30.408 --> 00:00:39.147
A paper, a version, a review, a consent and a moderation decision are all records, and the person who made each record signs it.

00:00:39.147 --> 00:00:41.649
You sign with a passkey on your own device.

00:00:41.649 --> 00:00:46.181
Your signing key never leaves the device, and the server never sees it.

00:00:46.181 --> 00:00:49.577
The server gets only your public key and your signatures.

00:00:49.577 --> 00:00:52.896
So the server cannot forge a record in your name.

00:00:52.896 --> 00:00:56.838
Idea two: nothing is edited. A signed version can never change.

00:00:56.838 --> 00:01:02.683
To update a paper, the author publishes a new version, linked to the version before it.

00:01:02.683 --> 00:01:06.046
Every version stays available at its own permanent ID.

00:01:06.046 --> 00:01:11.552
Corrections and retractions are new records. They never overwrite the old records.

00:01:11.552 --> 00:01:13.999
Idea three: anyone can check the record.

00:01:13.999 --> 00:01:21.616
When you open a paper, your browser checks its signatures, its history and its files, and shows the result as a badge.

00:01:21.616 --> 00:01:28.000
You can also download a paper, or the whole public archive, and check it offline with the Scholium verifier.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.528
You do not have to trust the server.

00:01:30.528 --> 00:01:34.346
Idea four: review is structured, sealed, and then revealed.

00:01:34.346 --> 00:01:40.763
A formal review scores a paper on six dimensions, and each score has a written justification.

00:01:40.763 --> 00:01:43.689
A review is sealed when the reviewer commits it.

00:01:43.689 --> 00:01:48.140
It is revealed only when a second reviewer commits, or when 21 days pass.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:51.040
So reviewers cannot influence each other.

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:54.450
Reviewers can stay anonymous and still be accountable.

00:01:54.450 --> 00:02:00.976
A fixed rule sets the status of the paper from its reviews, for example Under review or Reviewed.

00:02:00.976 --> 00:02:03.859
Idea five: AI helps, but it never decides.

00:02:03.859 --> 00:02:11.808
Every listed paper gets an AI pre-review from several specialist agents, and every finding must cite evidence.

00:02:11.808 --> 00:02:17.586
The report has a clear label: Advisory. Generated by AI agents. Not a peer review.

00:02:17.586 --> 00:02:21.509
The report can never change the status of a paper.

00:02:21.509 --> 00:02:24.293
Idea six: every decision shows its reason.

00:02:24.293 --> 00:02:27.739
Moderators decide only whether to list a submission.

00:02:27.739 --> 00:02:31.253
They never decide whether the science is right.

00:02:31.253 --> 00:02:35.149
Each decision cites a published policy clause and gives a reason.

00:02:35.149 --> 00:02:40.763
The decision is a public record, and the author can appeal it once to a panel of peers.

00:02:40.763 --> 00:02:47.714
Scholium deliberately has no like counts and no vote counts on papers, and no token or cryptocurrency.

00:02:47.714 --> 00:02:50.635
Reviews show quality, not popularity.

00:02:50.635 --> 00:02:52.903
What you can do depends on your trust tier.

00:02:52.903 --> 00:02:57.734
Your tier goes up when you link your identity, and when other members vouch for you.

00:02:57.734 --> 00:03:03.386
Readers read, search, check and cite. Members discuss and annotate. Authors publish.

00:03:03.386 --> 00:03:08.160
Reviewers write formal reviews. Moderators decide what is listed.

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:13.738
Now the life of a paper. The author signs and submits it. Every co-author consents.

00:03:13.738 --> 00:03:17.968
A safety scan checks the files. Then a moderator decides.

00:03:17.968 --> 00:03:23.474
A listed paper becomes public, with a place in the feed, in search, and a citable ID.

00:03:23.474 --> 00:03:29.120
If a moderator holds or rejects it, the author gets a reason and can appeal once.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:34.129
After listing, the paper gets an advisory AI pre-review and formal reviews.

00:03:34.129 --> 00:03:37.334
The authors reply and can publish a new version.

00:03:37.334 --> 00:03:41.808
The reviews then set a status, such as Reviewed or Contested.

00:03:41.808 --> 00:03:48.971
On a computer, the navigation bar holds the logo, search, Categories, Submit, notifications and your account.

00:03:48.971 --> 00:03:52.392
On a phone, the bar moves to the bottom of the screen.

00:03:52.392 --> 00:03:55.579
It holds Home, Search, Submit, Inbox and Me.

00:03:55.579 --> 00:03:58.834
This is the first release of Scholium, the community pilot.

00:03:58.834 --> 00:04:03.979
Some parts are not switched on yet, and the website tells you where they would appear.

00:04:03.979 --> 00:04:08.830
For example, public timestamps on a blockchain come in a later release.

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:11.905
Until then, every paper shows anchor pending.

00:04:11.905 --> 00:04:18.182
Today each paper also has one local copy, not copies at several independent operators.

00:04:18.182 --> 00:04:22.901
In short: people sign every record, and nobody edits a signed version.

00:04:22.901 --> 00:04:24.587
Anyone can check a paper.

00:04:24.587 --> 00:04:30.723
Reviews stay sealed until the reveal, AI only advises, and every decision gives its reason.

00:04:30.723 --> 00:04:33.280
Next, chapter two: your account.
